{"baseRefName":"main","body":"Calegari Question 13.2 asks whether an already-contact Godbillon–Vey connection form for the given taut foliation defines a tight contact structure. This package gives a complete affirmative proof in the closed oriented source setting: the constant pencil ω+sα remains contact, approaches the taut foliation, and transfers tightness through a finite smooth Gray path. An explicit smoothing argument treats C¹ forms without assuming a smooth foliation or a low-regularity Gray theorem.\n\nThe exact frozen proof passed a separate adversarial AI review with no mandatory correction. All 87 author controls replay byte-identically, and 89 independent exact diagnostics pass. The review and self-contained replay artifacts are included. The proof retains its original pending-review header solely to preserve the reviewed bytes; README and readiness record the final PASS.\n\nThis is conditional tightness only. The existence/weak-sign problem in Question 13.1 remains unresolved by this work. Classical Eliashberg–Thurston and Gray results are credited; historical priority and human peer review are unestablished. Two substantive approaches were used. No queue files are changed by this commit; campaign queue updates are coordinated separately.\n","files":[{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/QUEUE.md","additions":1,"deletions":1},{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/10300055/CANDIDATE.md","additions":125,"deletions":0},{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/10300055/README.md","additions":19,"deletions":0},{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/10300055/RESEARCH_LOG.md","additions":11,"deletions":0},{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/10300055/SOURCE_AUDIT.md","additions":23,"deletions":0},{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/10300055/frozen_artifacts.json","additions":11,"deletions":0},{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/10300055/readiness.json","additions":74,"deletions":0},{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/10300055/review/REVIEW.md","additions":67,"deletions":0},{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/10300055/review/author_replay/CANDIDATE.md","additions":125,"deletions":0},{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/10300055/review/author_replay/verification.json","additions":98,"deletions":0},{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/10300055/review/author_replay/verify.py","additions":183,"deletions":0},{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/10300055/review/independent_checks.py","additions":58,"deletions":0},{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/10300055/review/independent_results.json","additions":25,"deletions":0},{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/10300055/review/review_summary.json","additions":24,"deletions":0},{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/10300055/review/source_verification.json","additions":39,"deletions":0},{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/10300055/source_manifest.json","additions":39,"deletions":0},{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/10300055/turns.json","additions":19,"deletions":0},{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/10300055/upstream_record.json","additions":61,"deletions":0},{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/10300055/verification.json","additions":98,"deletions":0},{"path":"unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/10300055/verify.py","additions":183,"deletions":0}],"headRefName":"dot/math-10300055","headRefOid":"fb50facb2a7389bb272bbf0b5cbd80c24c79b992","isDraft":true,"number":97,"state":"OPEN","title":"Prove conditional tightness in Calegari Question 13.2","url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/pull/97"}
